 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the remaining tabs
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Decision: `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs `t1…tN` in order and resets `next` to N+1, so keys are always
+  contiguous, including after a later `openTab`.
+- Rejected: §stable-tab-keys (2026-09-02 → 2026-10-22) — assign each key once from `next`, never reuse or shift it,
+  and leave gaps on close (`t1,t3`). The maintainer overrode it on 2026-10-22.
+- Reason: maintainer decision, 2026-10-22.
+- Known cost: the key is the React key and the URL state. A close changes the key of every tab to the right of the
+  closed one, so React remounts them and they lose scroll position and unsaved form input (the behaviour behind the
+  2026-08-29 incident). URLs saved before a close can also point to different tabs afterwards.
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers keys", "open after close continues contiguously")
